.TH PLUGIN-QUALIFY-SYNC 1 "October 2026" "plugin-qualify" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
plugin-qualify-sync \- qualify every plugin on the machine once, and again when its files change
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plugin-qualify-sync
[\fB\-\-system\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-rejudge\fR | \fB\-\-memory\-only\fR | \fB\-\-adapter\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-distributed\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-\fIsetting\fR \fIvalue\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Finds every CLAP and LV2 plugin on the search paths and gives each new or changed one the quick
qualification: its pin, the real-time safety sweep, cost and latency, and its descriptor. A plugin whose
files hash as they did when it was qualified is skipped. The soak is not part of it, and each record says so.
.PP
Each plugin runs in processes of its own under a wall clock (\fBbudget_s\fR). One that crashes, hangs or is
refused is recorded as such, and the run goes on to the next. The pin file plugin-hostd loads as
\fBpin_file\fR is written whole after every plugin. When plugin-hostd answers on its command port, the
changed pins are also sent to it with \fBpin_set\fR; while a controller holds that port, they apply at its
next start.
.PP
A plugin that is no longer found, or whose bytes changed, leaves nothing behind: its pin, its qualification
record and the probe output beside it, and its descriptor are removed, and a running plugin-hostd is told with
\fBpin_clear\fR. Nothing is removed in a run that could not read a plugin bundle.
.PP
Before a plugin is measured, the distributed records of the machine class are read
(/usr/share/omx/qualification/\fIhost class\fR/\fIset\fR/, installed by a package of a plugin set). A record
applies only when every file it names is installed with the SHA-256 it recorded; the plugin is then not measured,
the record is copied into the qualification directory with the set's provenance, and the run says
\fBdistributed: \fIuri\fB: applied (\fIset\fB)\fR. A record whose files differ is ignored
(\fBdistributed: \fIuri\fB: binary differs\fR) and the plugin is measured. The host class is \fBhost_class\fR,
else the board's own from the device tree (\fBrpi5\fR on a Raspberry Pi 5).
.PP
The last line on standard output counts the plugins: qualified, skipped, refused, crashed, hung,
unmeasured, taken from the distributed records, and the seconds the run took.
.PP
After an upgrade the figures in the records are carried across and judged again rather than measured again:
\fB\-\-rejudge\fR, \fB\-\-memory\-only\fR and \fB\-\-adapter\-only\fR below.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-system
The directories of a system service, under /var/lib/omx.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Qualify every plugin again, recorded or not; no distributed record is read.
.TP
.B \-\-rejudge
Measure nothing. Derive every record's verdicts, tiers and pin word again from the figures it holds, under this version's
rules; the figures, the probe output and who took them are not changed, and the record's \fBrejudged\fR block says when
they were measured and by which runner. A verdict that needs a run the record lacks (an LV2 plugin's
\fBomx\-clap\-host\fR verdict and its run under the LV2 adapter) reads \fBunknown\fR, its pin has no verdict word, and
the record owes that run. A record of changed bytes or with no readable probe output is left alone and owes a
qualification. What is owed is printed, kept under \fBowes\fR in the record, and listed in \fIqualify\-owed.json\fR beside
the state file. A second run changes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-memory\-only
Measure only the footprint of each plugin the state holds, at 48000 Hz in blocks of 128 and 256 frames, through the probe
runner under the usual limits, and merge it into its record. No other figure changes. It judges nothing; run
\fB\-\-rejudge\fR after it. A plugin already merged is skipped; with \fB\-\-force\fR it is measured again.
A leak needs a soak and is not measured: the record says so under \fBnotMeasured\fR, and the last line says so.
.TP
.B \-\-adapter\-only
Run each LV2 plugin the state holds only under omx\-clap\-host's LV2 adapter, at 48000 Hz in blocks of 128 and 256 frames,
each cell on a private JACK server (\fBjackd\fR must be installed; the live server is never used), and put the block in its
record with the host versions. No other figure changes; run \fB\-\-rejudge\fR after it. A crash or a timeout is the
plugin's recorded outcome. It refuses to start when a host does not say its version. A plugin done at these host
versions is skipped; with \fB\-\-force\fR it is run again.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-distributed
Read no distributed record.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
The settings file (default /etc/omx/qualify.conf).
.TP
.BI \-\- "setting value"
Any setting of the file, as a flag: \fB\-\-budget\-s 30\fR for \fBbudget_s 30\fR.
\fBplugin-qualify-sync \-\-help\fR lists them with their defaults.
.SH FILES
/etc/omx/qualify.conf, /var/lib/omx/qualified.pins, /var/lib/omx/qualification, /var/lib/omx/descriptors,
/var/lib/omx/qualify-sync.json, /var/lib/omx/qualify-owed.json, /usr/share/omx/qualification
.PP
plugin-qualify-sync.path watches the standard plugin folders. The folders the settings file adds
(\fBlv2_path\fR, \fBlv2_extra\fR, \fBclap_path\fR, \fBclap_extra\fR) are written into a drop-in of that unit by a
systemd generator at boot and at every \fBsystemctl daemon\-reload\fR.
.PP
The path unit sees a bundle appear in a watched folder at once, but not a bundle's files being replaced in
place (an upgrade). A plugin from a package is still caught at once, by the distribution's install triggers
over the standard folders; an unpackaged bundle replaced in place in an extra folder is caught at the next run
of plugin-qualify-sync.timer, at most about 15 minutes after the change.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 the run went through, whatever the verdicts; 2 it could not run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR plugin-qualify (1),
.BR plugin-hostd (1)
